    "title": "The Story of the Bicycle",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "The first bicycle was made in 1817 by a German man called Karl von Drais. It had two wheels, but it had no pedals. The rider sat on it and pushed the ground with his feet. People called it a running machine, and it was used mainly by rich people for fun."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "In the 1860s, French inventors added pedals to the front wheel. This bicycle was called a boneshaker because the road was bumpy and the wheels were made of wood and metal. It was heavy and uncomfortable, so it was not popular with many people."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Later, in 1870, a bicycle with a very big front wheel appeared. It was faster, but it was also dangerous. The rider sat high above the ground, and falling from it could cause serious accidents. Women and older people did not use it."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "The big change came in the 1880s. A new design, called the safety bicycle, had two wheels of the same size and a chain to the back wheel. It was easier to ride, and it was much safer. Soon, millions of people were buying bicycles. Today, there are more than one billion bicycles in the world, and many cities are building special roads for them."
      }
    ]
  },
